Scale ruling closes the event-loop question. The agent noted the `process_and_close_socket` virtual (:2388) as a potential subclass seam for hand-off without patching vendored source.

The subagent returned a comprehensive survey (all sources fetched 2026-11-01). Key findings: field is bimodal (proxy/LB tier ~60s, origin tier 5–130s). Three claims refuted at source: Envoy `keep-alive: timeout=0` (no such string in `conn_manager_impl.cc`), nginx half-timeout stamp (re-arms, never stamps), Node `serverClosing` (not in tree). The real industry anti-race mechanism is Node's `keepAliveTimeoutBuffer` (`lib/internal/http/server.js`, v24.6/v22.19): advertise deadline earlier than actual close (announce 65s, close at 66s). Tomcat's poller (`pollerThreadCount` default 1 on Linux) is the canonical hand-off pattern — confirmed unreachable in cpp-httplib.

Synthesis presented: (1) `--keep-alive-timeout-sec` default 60, (2) rewrite the native `Keep-Alive:` header to advertise earlier than close (Node's mechanism, ~10 lines, public API), (3) the ½-threshold `Connection: close` stamp demoted to fallback for header-ignoring clients (httpx, SDK pools), (4) `--max-http-clients N` surfaces the two-tier pool (Tomcat-shaped). One real decision left: stamp in or out.

