Falsified the "client retries" assumption. Verified undici honors Keep-Alive: timeout= (pool TTL = min(N−2s, 10min)), so undici never meets the kill. The error source is Bun's own HTTP/1.1 client (opencode ships on Bun, not undici). Structural inversion: for header-blind clients the correct server TTL is max(client pool TTLs) + margin, not a survey median. Dispatched subagent c9c2c812 to investigate Bun/opencode's keep-alive contract.

## 11355-11381 · Converged Infernix keep-alive PR to configuration-only tier A
A Paseo subagent ("Investigate opencode HTTP keepalive", c9c2c812) returned a source-level investigation of opencode v1.19.5's HTTP client stack. The findings settled every open design question for the Infernix keep-alive PR:

**Client ground truth (Bun v1.4.4 native Rust HTTP/1.1 client; opencode sets no HTTP options itself):**
- Pool idle TTL: 300 s, minute-wheel granularity (up to ~360 s) — `src/http/lib.rs:332`, `HTTPContext.rs arm_idle_timeout`
- Honors `Keep-Alive: timeout=`: **No** — header switch parses only CL/CT/TE/`Connection`/etc. (`src/http/lib.rs:5012-5085`)
- Honors `Connection: close`: Yes — sole retirement signal (`allow_keepalive=false`)
- Probe before reuse: Yes — real peek (`queued_input` Eof/Data/Error → discard) in `HTTPContext::find_in`
- POST written-then-silence retried by client: **No** — `retries_on_close()` requires `method.is_idempotent()`, POST excluded; AI SDK `maxRetries: 0` (`lib.rs:2231-2247`)
- Retried by opencode session layer: Yes-if-matched — regex incl. `fetch failed|socket hang up|econnreset`, 5 tries ≥2 s (`session/retry.ts`), but the Rust→JS string for `ConnectionClosed` is unverified

I presented four design consequences: (1) the buffer idea is dead (nobody consumes the advertisement), (2) the stamp is the only server→client retirement signal Bun respects, (3) server timeout must exceed ~360 s on this box (PR default stays 60 per maintainer merge bar), (4) bonus: opencode hard-aborts at 300 s to first response byte (`headerTimeout`).

U: we don't care about "@opencode-ai/llm" infernix only supports anthropic and openai-compatible

That closed the `experimentalNativeLlm` gap by scope — the traced Bun stack is the whole client population. I committed the design doc (commit a67ce6c) and then proposed shrinking the PR: the stamp (`keep_alive_retirement.{h,cpp}`) was 175 of 242 LOC; everything else (timeout flag, pool headroom, cap, tests, docs) is ~67 lines. Presented three tiers: A (configuration-only, no stamp), B (stamp inlined), C (current full).

The user then asked whether an instant `ConnectionClosed` 503 reply would work as an alternative. I traced it mechanically (set_pre_routing_handler → Handled → fabricated response with `Connection: close` → Bun honors it → session-layer retry on 500 always fires) but showed it's dominated by the stamp at identical code cost: it manufactures a failure the stamp avoids (the request was reachable), and the "abandon a doomed generation" argument doesn't exist because httplib's idle timer runs only between requests.

U: what "process" means? it's still allows connection to do whole sequence, what's the point to retire connection at this point if connection got reused and keepalive generally should just resets

This dismantled the stamp's last defense. "Process" means serve normally — and serving resets the keep-alive timer on both sides. A late reuse at ½T isn't "a connection about to die"; the reuse IS the life signal. The stamp's real rationale was statistical (punish a demonstrably healthy connection for a race the probe + FIN drain already absorb), and it covers zero of the true residual (the in-flight FIN/RST window has no response to stamp on). The 503 variant inherited the same futility.

PR is tier A by elimination: timeout flag (default 60, guard [1,3600]) + `--max-http-clients` cap + headroom + tests + docs. ~67 LOC, zero custom protocol behavior. Committed the realigned doc (commit 8993b93), started implementation on the Mac branch.

