The ruling: the advertised timeout is not a lie because (1) an idle connection is always held the full advertised N — the stamp physically cannot fire without a request riding it; (2) a late-reuse connection gets its complete request/response exchange before the close arrives; (3) RFC 9112 §9.5's timeout speaks about closure "with no activity" and our idle floor is exactly N. The fourth delta (header rewrite) dies. Upstream's native `Keep-Alive: timeout=60, max=…` stays untouched.

On the pool formula, the user ruled a third path replacing both competing schemes (upstream's fixed +64 headroom and our stock-by-default):

