## 12134-12162 · 2026-11-02 · Designed append-only effort-switch cache strategy
U: well, now i'm wondering, switching reasoning effort seems to invalidate whole cache, since it seems to change system block, would it be reasonable to not invalidate whole chat due to system block change especially if it's a jinja template addition?

I explained the invalidation is causal-attention math (every KV entry after the system block was computed attending over those tokens), not a relaxable policy. The design principle is "stable prefix first, volatile content last" — move effort instructions from the system head to a trailing block. I proposed append-only supersession: emit the block once, leave it in history, append a new one on change.

U: thing is, even if we set block into volatile position, it will move each turn which might invalidate cache for everything apart system prompt

I corrected this: the failure mode belongs to a *moving* implementation, not tail placement per se. The fix is append-only — emit once, never re-render. The block is just another append event in the already-append-only agent loop.

U: "On an effort **change**" and you want to say that it can be implemented in jinja?

I admitted I glossed it — jinja is a pure function of (messages, tools, kwargs) per request with no memory. Append-on-change needs server cooperation: the serve stamps effort-per-turn into the session record (same rail as preserve-thinking), and the template reads turn_meta to diff. The ladder: jinja alone buys frozen-head or tail-re-emitted-every-request; the "nothing ever moves" design is ~10 lines server-side metadata stamp plus template logic.

U: so, to test it out we have to make a control runs with different effort levels, then do control runs with chat switching efforts in a middle

I designed the battery: fixed-effort arms (F-xhigh, F-low, F-medium × 8 turns), switch arms (SW-down, SW-up at turn 5), and a no-op switch control (SW-blank). Metrics: cache%+TTFT on first post-switch request (cost claim), reasoning tokens per turn after switch (obedience claim), request N+2 cache% (recovery check). One implementation unknown: which wire field opencode uses for effort.

U: what i mean is that if system message is a part of a conversation, opencode should persist it as every other client should, otherwise it will generate cache bust on each turn since system message appeared

I verified from the local session DB: only two roles are ever persisted — user (14,023 rows) and assistant (83,399). System messages are not conversation data in opencode; they're re-assembled per request from config/AGENTS.md/plugin hooks. This validates the concern with an exact boundary: derived-per-request content is cache-safe iff it's a pure function of immutable inputs. The only channel giving "persisted, never moves" is the user-turn append.

The conversation also covered: behavioral fingerprint detection (user's "goofy/woody" idea — rejected as probabilistic classifier when the fact is one field away in the request body), system message protocol semantics (nothing in the protocol ignores them; the "ignored" reputation comes from model-era training + client normalization layers), and the conclusion that the server-side stamp is the primary design because it works regardless of client cooperation.

