## 12196-12216 · Verified Infernix thinking-preservation mechanism is client-owned on chat/completions
The user asked what the Sharp model card's "thinking preservation" paragraph means mechanically. I explained the three-layer chain (model trained with thinking in-content → client must resend reasoning_content → template must render it), then the user pushed to verify whether Infernix actually restores thinking server-side.

U: what i meant is if client omits thinking, infernix still restores it, no?

I ran a live test: sent a turn producing thinking, then resent the same history without any reasoning_content field in the assistant message, checking whether prompt size included the prior thinking block. Result: 235 tokens rendered whether or not the session previously saw thinking — nothing was restored. On `/v1/chat/completions`, Infernix is stateless: `chat_template.cpp:358` injects `reasoning_content` only from what the message carries in, no server-side text store behind it.

This falsified my earlier claim (made in this same conversation) that Infernix's `preserve thinking` log line indicated server-side storage. The log line is just the client's per-request boolean being echoed (parsed at `openai_chat_request.cpp:871`, passed to the template context). In our daily, thinking survives because **opencode itself is the persistence layer** — 82,110 `reasoning` parts in the local DB, one per assistant turn, resent on every request.

The serve does have an audit mechanism (`chat_template.cpp:564-575`): after generating an open assistant turn, it renders a probe (same history + empty user message) and compares token prefixes to verify the template retains that turn's thinking as frozen history — guarding fidelity, not supplying content.

The Responses API (`/v1/responses`, `openai_responses_state.cpp`) is where Infernix genuinely stores and restores server-side — that's the stateful endpoint. Chat/completions clients (including opencode) are on their own honesty.

The user then hypothesized that "no cache bust on stripped request" meant the server re-added thinking silently. I checked serve logs: both stripped arms hit exactly 201 tokens (85.5%) — the stable head shared by every request with that opening. If the server had re-added thinking, cache would have been ~235 ≈ 100%. The response does carry `reasoning_content` (60 chars read from r1) — no client-rejection suppression. The correct reading: there was nothing to bust because the stripped request never got past the common head in either arm.

## 12217-12232 · Designed cache-safe reasoning_effort session-state architecture
User directed a prior-art investigation into cache-safe reasoning effort switching for Qwen3.5+ models, dispatching a deepseek-v4.1-flash explorer agent (6e37f468) to survey mechanism families: tail-rendered effort directives, out-of-band budgets, cache architectures surviving mid-sequence edits, and production API behavior on effort flips.

U: "well, the proper solution should be openai-compatible implementation, not opencode specific, for example server might actually just hold specific position where it injects new reasoning effort and store this data unless kv cache of a session is invalidated"

I elaborated the design: server stores `reasoning_effort` per session (last-write-wins), omission inherits stored value instead of falling to boot default, frozen injection position + stored value makes per-turn rendering byte-identical by construction, lifetime tied to session KV invalidation. Noted Infernix's Responses API path (`openai_responses_state.cpp`) already has the session-state scaffolding — extending it to reasoning_effort is plumbing on an existing path.

Explorer returned 23 sourced rows. Headline: no surveyed OpenAI-compatible engine stores effort as session state — vLLM, SGLang, llama.cpp, DashScope, OpenAI Responses chain all treat it per-request. The design is novel at the engine layer. Key findings bearing on the architecture:

- Qwen3.8 renders the effort paragraph into the system message at token 0 (template L45-85) — session storage alone doesn't fix change cost; the stored value must move out of the head or every flip is full re-prefill.
- Omission ≠ "no directive" in the upstream template: omitted effort → xhigh paragraph; explicit `medium` → no paragraph at all. The state machine needs three states (unset/inherit, explicit-set/update, never-conflate).
- Qwen3.5/3.6 already render thinking control at the generation-prompt tail — tail slot is vendor house style, not an invention.
- SGLang `session_params.offset` is the nearest existing primitive to "specific position the server holds" — a real mid-sequence edit surviving prefix cache, but token-splice plumbing not effort state.
- Out-of-band budgets (vLLM `thinking_token_budget`, SGLang strict-thinking logit filter, llama.cpp reasoning-budget) are the one family where changing the dial is free by construction (zero prompt bytes), but can only cap depth, not instruct it.

Converged shape: session-stored effort (three-state machine) + tail-rendering at the generation-prompt slot, lifetime tied to session KV. Every ingredient has precedent (OpenAI breakpoint doctrine, Qwen's own tail toggle, SGLang offset splice) but the combination is unmeasured — the switch-battery experiment would bank first data. User directed writing the report to `docs/research/` with no follow-ups needed.

