U: it depends on model i guess, even though 3.8 still inherits 3.5 general architecture, thinking preservation is appeared in 3.8+

I fetched the official 3.8 template and confirmed the split: 3.8-Flash-Next keeps completed-turn thinking by default, gated on `preserve_thinking` (undefined → true, so retention is the shipped default and stripping is opt-in). 3.5-27B has no `preserve_thinking` variable at all — the same render site is gated on position only, so completed turns are stripped unconditionally. 3.5 was trained consuming history without old thinking blocks; 3.8 changed the conditioning contract. Two consequences: (1) opencode's resending of `reasoning_content` isn't wasted fuel on our daily — the 3.8 template consumes it for every completed turn; (2) the official 3.8 template has no medium paragraph either — only xhigh and low branches assign `reasoning_instructions`; medium falls through to empty string. Updated doc rows 11–12 with verbatim gates, the 3.5-strips/3.8-retains generation split, and medium-paragraph provenance. Committed ca92bf8.

U: "exists only in our NInfer-derived fork" because it's i am who added it, because empty paragraph doesn't define anything while this one seems to be exact middle of xhigh and low

I corrected the doc's attribution: the medium paragraph is owner-authored (not a fork feature inherited from elsewhere), added because the empty upstream paragraph defines nothing and the owner's text represents the exact middle of xhigh and low. Updated headline 98 and gaps bullet to remove the stale "upstream report owed" phrasing.

## 12263-12333 · Validated tail-rendered reasoning effort via gate-1 battery
Corrected four spots in the reasoning-effort doc (commit 3e4ef74): the medium paragraph is owner-authored (rationale recorded verbatim from the user — empty paragraph defines nothing, the written one is the exact midpoint between xhigh and low directives), its ns_state condition shipped broken from day one, and the "upstream report owed" claim was deleted everywhere (upstream's template contains no ns_state line and no medium branch at all).

The user then asked about official template cache behavior for reasoning effort changes. I provided a matrix showing that Qwen3.8-Flash-Next places the reasoning_effort paragraph at token 0 (system message head), making any effort change a full re-prefill; Qwen3.5/3.6 places only the binary enable_thinking at the generation prompt tail (cache-free); Qwen3 uses client-appended /think /no_think markers (cache-free, append-only). I initially wrote "the official templates never do the thing that would bust the cache" — the user caught this as wrong (12270: "then why the f this line exists") and I retracted: the correct claim is that templates never mutate mid-history, but head placement of volatile content (3.8's effort paragraph) does bust the cache.

U: "what if reasoning effort(or all of the template head-generated content) is moved into separate cache block and when client changes reasoning effort it generates another block which takes place only for past-change tokens while older are calculated against old block?"

I analyzed the block-swap proposal mechanically (paged KV already composes from non-contiguous blocks; two implementable variants: keep old head + append new directive at tail = supersession-by-recency, or drop old head keeping its downstream KV = conditioning discontinuity) and behaviorally (stale-conditioned KV residue, DFlash2 off-distribution risk, prefix-hash identity assumptions). Identified prior art as adjacent but never this (SGLang session_params.offset, Azure cache breakpoints). Proposed gate-1: simulate the tail-directive shape over HTTP on the daily serve with scripted history, no engine change needed.

The user said "well, try it." I built a 20-shot battery (4 arms × 5 shots, temp 0, ~33k-token scripted history from httplib.h slice + 3 canned Q/A pairs, probe = seeded-bug Rust function). Arms: A (xhigh head, baseline), B (true low head switch, cold re-prefill), C (xhigh head untouched, low paragraph verbatim appended inside the newest user turn), D (xhigh head, unrelated 33-token filler in newest user turn as confound control). Launched detached with a 2-min Paseo heartbeat.

Results (commit 8c8ae83): C recovers 88.2% of the head-switch effect ((6168−2044)/(6168−1490)). D is bit-identical to A (6168/6239/23731) — filler has zero effect, the directive's semantics drive the change. The price of tail placement: +554 reasoning tokens over true-low (head xhigh still pulls). Cache side: C costs +26 prompt tokens, 11.5s warm walls, prefix never busted; B pays cold re-prefill of full 32.9k on first shot. Instrument bonus: every arm bit-identical across all 5 shots (mean=median) — the #2562 non-determinism came from model-generated history, not the engine.

The user pointed out I buried results in toolcalls (12327: "you've buried results in toolcalls"), so I re-presented the full table and verdict in the response text. The user then clarified the test shape (12329: "so, test was like adding paragraph in a middle?") — I confirmed it was at the tail (inside the newest user message, strictly after every cached token), not mid-history insertion. The user's final lead (12331: "take a look at infernix log btw it does shows reasoning effort if i remember it right") was being investigated at chunk end.

Gate 2 (open): history genuinely generated under xhigh across live turns, then tail directive appended — tests the stale-conditioned residue term that scripted history cannot reach.

## 12334-12356 · Discovered thinking-budget censoring in effort battery; launched definitive uncapped re-run
I was verifying the effort-steering battery's arm construction against the serve's own operational log. The `started` lines (operational_log.cpp:181, via `requested_effort_name` at :108) independently confirmed the wire values: arm A = `thinking xhigh, budget 6,144`, arm B = `thinking low, budget 6,144`, arms C+D = `thinking xhigh` (head never moved — C's tail directive was purely message content). The panel also renders the three-state taxonomy (explicit level / omitted → "template default" / off) that the session-state machine must keep distinct.

U: when session ends infernix shows statistics, with "done"

Pulling the `done` lines for req#101–131 exposed an instrument defect: **arms A and D saturated the serve's thinking budget, not the model's patience.** Every A/D shot logged `thinking 6,144/6,144, control 25` — the serve caps thinking at 75% of `max_tokens`, and I sent `max_tokens: 8192` → budget 6,144. The "xhigh baseline" (6,168 output) was censored at the ceiling; the model wanted to think more and got truncated. B (1,490/6,144) and C (2,044/6,144) finished naturally under the cap — those two numbers are clean. Consequence: the 88.2% recovery fraction is computed against a truncated denominator; true uncapped xhigh &gt; 6,168, so the real tail-directive recovery is **lower** than 88.2%. Direction survives, magnitude needs the uncapped baseline.

Launched uncapped re-run (arms A2/D2 at `max_tokens: 32768` → budget 24,576, same shape as real opencode traffic), heartbeat watching.

U: yeah, re-run, but i also meant to check whether C ended with different effort level

Decoded `control N` from source (operational_log.cpp:309-316): it prints only when `outcome.thinking.injected_tokens != 0` — tokens the engine injected to force the thinking block closed. The done lines answer the question directly: A and D ended **engine-forced** (budget exhausted, 25 control tokens injected); B and C ended **voluntarily** (model closed `&lt;/think&gt;` itself, no injection). C's termination mode matches low, not xhigh — a behavioral fingerprint independent of token count. The tail directive changed when the model *chooses* to stop thinking, not just how much it emitted before truncation.

U: well, you can run cleaner test

Killed the partial A2/D2 re-run and launched the definitive battery: all four arms under identical uncapped conditions, every shot ending voluntarily, plus serve-log termination evidence for each.

## 12357-12402 · Clean effort battery overturns capped-run headline; truncation was manufacturing the control
The clean (uncapped) effort battery completed 20/20 and overturned my own prior headline. The capped run's "88.2% exact control" was a truncation artifact: both the xhigh and filler arms hit the same 6,144-token ceiling, so they looked bit-identical not because the filler had no effect but because the cap erased the difference.

Early surprise at 3/20: uncapped xhigh closes voluntarily at 5,107 reasoning tokens (bit-identical across warm shots; cold prefill shot = 6,012 — cache-epoch divergence consistent with #2562), well under the old 6,144 cap. The original run's "budget saturated" ending was the control-injection path distorting generation, not the model's natural volume.

Final table (5 shots per arm): A xhigh head = 5107×3, 6012×2 reasoning, 223 answer chars, 31.1s wall. B low head = 1490×5, 248 chars, 6.7s. C tail directive = 2332×5, 543 chars, 13.5s. D filler tail = 2761×5, 236 chars, 16.7s.

What broke: D ≠ A uncapped. 33 tokens of meaningless filler prose in the tail drops thinking 5107→2761 (−46%). Any tail perturbation resamples the greedy trajectory into a different thinking-volume basin; basins for the same head span 2761–6012. The "bit-identical filler control" was the truncation itself.

What survives: within-prompt determinism holds (B/C/D each bit-identical across shots); the directive has a genuine effect on behavior shape (C's answers are 543 chars low-style vs D's 236 and A's 223 — filler didn't change answer style, only the low-effort paragraph did); true low-head (1490) remains clearly below every xhigh basin.

What's weakened: the volume claim. C=2332 vs D=2761 is a 15% increment attributable to the directive's words over generic tail content; the 76.7% recovery figure against A is contaminated by basin-sampling noise. One filler sample can't map the null distribution. Proposed filler sweep (4–5 neutral tail texts × 5 shots, ~25 min serve) to decide whether C's 2,332 is a real directive effect or basin luck.

Doc corrected to honest state (capped 88.2% → truncation artifact → clean numbers), committed e920b90. Heartbeat retired.

U: collect start/done logs as well
Pulled started + done lines for all 20 clean-battery requests from the serve log, banked as `docs/research/evidence/effort-clean-2026-11-02.log` (commit 1161c3f), linked from the battery section. 40 lines: started + done per shot, tagged by arm/shot. Pairing disambiguated by the done-line `thinking X/24,576` census (req# counter collides across restarts; three foreign 32,768 requests share the window — arm identified by token count, unique per arm; 20/20 match the client jsonl). All shots `stop token` (voluntary close, no `control N` anywhere in the clean run); cache 100% on every warm shot including C/D (tail additions never busted the prefix); TTFT ~50ms warm across all four arms.

