## 12403-12452 · Gate 2 effort-switch battery: design, execution, and initial harvest
The Gate 1 mechanism verdict (xhigh-labeled request closing like low without busting cache) was already in the record. The user directed continuation and flagged the key remaining gap:
U: "go on, the only real problem is that cache is validated against post-jinja conversion as it seems"
I confirmed this structurally — the engine only consumes token ids (jinja renders → tokenizer → engine; block cache hashes those ids), so `cache 100.0%` validates identity post-render, never at the message layer. The battery fixed rendered bytes itself (every shot re-sent the identical string), proving block-matching works but proving nothing about the production property: whether per-turn re-rendering keeps historical bytes stable when effort changes.

I designed Gate 2 and banked it as smart note #75. The user couldn't see notes without expanding, so I read it back verbatim and presented the full three-arm design (GW correct switch, GB wrong-switch falsifier, GC control).

The user then specified the sweep targets and gave the architectural model:
U: "try to check it with medium low and reasoning off, how i see it is that server has to hold initial and all transient effort switches and inject jinja generated prose at positions when switch happens"
I mapped this to the append-only supersession shape: server holds `initial_effort` + ordered `[(turn_pos, effort)]`, jinja injects each switch's prose permanently at its position, per-turn re-render byte-stable by construction. The user's model simulated client-side: frozen history + directive appended at switch turns (client controls resubmitted bytes).

The user reinforced the measurement principle:
U: "yeah, don't forget start and done logs are main source of truth, whether effort has been changed or not"

I rewrote the runner (`/tmp/effort_g2.py`) with correct append-only semantics (directive baked permanently into the switch turn's saved user message, never re-rendered into older turns) and launched seven arms against the daily serve under live traffic: GC_control (9 turns no switch), SM_med (medium prose @7), SL_low (low prose @7), SO_off (off-draft prose @7), SD_stack (double-switch xhigh→low→off), GW_wrong (falsifier: directive retro-injected into all past turns @7), BH_head (true `reasoning_effort:low` from turn 7 — the contrast row).

First heartbeat (36/65): SO_off post-switch reasoning collapsed to 216 tokens — the tail off-draft beat the head's structural `&lt;think&gt;` prefill. Second heartbeat (65/65, zero errors): the falsifier fired (GW_wrong showed cache% collapse at turn 7). Initial harvest found two join artifacts: (1) arm windows leaked foreign non-stream requests (extra started lines shifted GW's turn pairing — `srv-think` vs `cli-think` mismatch is the tell), (2) tail arms sent no `reasoning_effort` field so the server prints `thinking template default` (omitted) vs explicit `thinking low` (BH) — my regex missed the space. Re-joining by content (req# ↔ arm,turn matched on prompt_tokens + done-timestamp ±3s) to fix both.

## 12453-12495 · Gate-2 effort-switch battery completed with reference arms and lever tests
Reported and committed the gate-2 results (663df1f): 65/65 requests joined to serve started/done pairs, thinking census server==client on every row. The switch-turn table covers six arms on identical 6-turn xhigh-generated history with a real 1,729-token turn-2 thinking block, switching at t7. Key rows: GC control (381 thinking, 98.9% cache), SM→medium (232, 97.8%), SL→low (151, 98.2%), SO→off draft prose (115, 98.3%), GW retro-inject wrong impl (312, **0.0%** — the falsifier), BH true head flip (291, **0.0%**). SD_stack (all transient switches): low@7→151@99.8%, off@10→109@99.0%, t11→42@99.6% — both directives held permanently, cache never moved.

