## 12496-12528 · 2026-11-02 · Validated enable_thinking:false as cache-neutral off lever via template head decoupling
The user asked whether `add_generation_prompt` is per-message and whether we can pass the thinking setting properly as a protocol field. I verified against the serve's actual render calls: `add_generation_prompt` is a single per-request flag on one whole-conversation render (`chat_template.h:105`, serve always sets `true`); the engine does extra truncated renders (`chat_template.cpp:~460`) only to prove message boundaries — those pass `add_generation_prompt=false` on prefixes. Plus a per-session frontier: after each reply, the render is truncated at the next `&lt;|im_start|&gt;`.

I then mapped the three sites where `enable_thinking` bites in the template: head paragraph (L64, gated `if enable_thinking undefined or true` — busts from byte ~0), terse lead (L77, `_thinking_on` — same head region), and generation prefill (L222, `&lt;think&gt;\n` vs `&lt;think&gt;\n\n&lt;/think&gt;\n\n` — tail only, after every cached byte). Past-turn thinking (L171) is gated by `preserve_thinking` only, not `enable_thinking`. The conclusion: off doesn't need prose at all — the empty-prefill is the trained signal itself — it's currently only un-injectable because the two head gates make it also a head rewrite.

Proposed fix: decouple the head from `enable_thinking` in the template fork (head renders the session-frozen directive unconditionally; L222 keeps reacting). ~2 lines across two files. Then per-request `enable_thinking:false` becomes a pure tail change.

