## 12529-12555 · Converged reasoning_effort cache-safety engine design ruling
Continuation of the reasoning_effort cache-safety investigation. I explained the falsifier mechanism: the offref patch moved thinking-state gates off the head but the paragraph block still keys on `resolved_reasoning_effort`, so per-request effort switching rebuilds the head from byte 0 (req#26 at 0.0% cache), then re-warms to 99.4% on subsequent turns. The design boundary: effort is a head variable by construction (its instruction text renders in the head paragraph), so per-request `reasoning_effort` stays cache-hostile even on offref.

U: "well we don't have to enforce anything from my perspective, it should just detect that cache bust happens due to jinja profile and reset session state"

This dissolved the enforcement machinery. I proposed the detect-and-reset shape: wire stays stateless, per-session epoch (message fingerprints + rendered-token frontier), divergence classified by message-diff (extension-with-token-change = jinja-profile cause; non-extension = client rewrite), logged, epoch-reset, re-prefilled.

U: "not _cheap_ and i don't tell that it should be cheap, we're just introduce non-breaking behaviour which makes best effort at preserving the cache, not a silver bullet if jinja template fights against it"

This set the grading: correctness is the hard guarantee (no template can break the engine), cache preservation is the soft property (best-effort, templates that cooperate earn 98-99%, ones that don't pay re-prefill and show up in the log). I drafted the converged-shape section.

U: "you have to define what specific mechanisms are assumed to be used and i don't understand why it stopped session state, if we don't connect to session - we loose temporal data of an effort switch position"

I had over-corrected — dropping session state entirely. The switch positions ARE session data: "inject prose at the position where the switch happened" requires the server to remember the ordered switch list. I restored the session record (initial_effort + ordered [(turn_pos, effort)]) as the source of truth for directive injection positions, with the correctness guarantee independent of it.

U: "what we also didn't define is that session can be reversed, so if head comes with shorter amount of turns, or with the same turn as effort flip happens - it should be handled gracefully"

I added the reversibility layer: fingerprints computed over client wire messages (not rendered prompt), switches anchored to message identity (not turn index), rewind = truncate at first non-matching fingerprint + drop switches whose anchors are gone, same-turn flip = last-write-wins within anchor slot, branch/regenerate = new lineage. The invariant: rendering = f(client-domain history, anchored switch list).

User approved ("sounds about right"). I committed the ruling into `docs/research/reasoning-effort-cache-safety-2026-11-02.md` §Engine design (commit 020ce21) and parked a smart note (#76) for the dreamer.

